@Tutorial(time: 8) {
    @Intro(title: "Xogot for Mac Tour") {
        Get oriented with Xogot's Mac-native Godot development environment.

        @Image(source: mac-tour-editor-overview.png)
    }

    @Section(title: "Welcome to Your Workspace") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Xogot for Mac keeps the Godot project model, but presents it as a native Mac
            workspace with a source list, centered editor surface, and inspector panes.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                When you first open Xogot, you will see the Projects screen.
                
                From this screen, you can open or create a new project, find recent projects,
                or clone a Git repository. 

                @Image(source: mac-tour-projects.png)
            }

            @Step {
                If you don't have an existing project to start with or you don't know where
                to begin, you can access Xogot tutorials, videos, and sample projects by
                opening **Learning Center** under the **Window** menu.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-learning-center.png)
            }

            @Step {
                After you open a project, Xogot shows the main editor workspace.

                As in Godot, the scene tree and file pad share the left side of the 
                workspace. The selected scene, script, or game view opens in the center. 
                Inspector, signal, node, import, and plugin details appear on the right.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-editor-overview.png)
            }

            @Step {
                Use the toolbar to switch between 3D, 2D, script, and running-game
                contexts.

                The selected run target appears in the center of the toolbar. **Local
                Editor** runs the project in the editor on your Mac.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-local-editor.png)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Navigate Through Your Project") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Xogot's left sidebar is organized like Xcode's navigator area, with
            dedicated tabs for browsing files, searching, reviewing breakpoints, and
            inspecting a running project.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                The first tab after the Scene and Files tab is the Find Navigator.
                Use this tab to search across files in your project.
                
                The Find Navigator can also be used for find and replace. The default
                mode is text search, and it also supports regular expressions.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-code-search.png)
            }
            @Step {
                The Report Navigator keeps track of previous build and run logs.
                
                Use it to compare console output, debugging output, and build results
                across changes.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-report-navigator.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Open the Breakpoints navigator to review active and disabled breakpoints
                across project scripts.

                Selecting a breakpoint opens the script and line that owns it. You can
                also delete breakpoints or enable and disable them directly from here.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-breakpoints.png)
            }

            @Step {
                Open the Debug navigator while the project is running.

                This view shows live monitors for frame time, memory, objects, rendering,
                and other runtime counters.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-debug-navigator.png)
            }


        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Settings, Commands, Scenes, and Targets") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Xogot also includes Mac-native controls for configuring the editor,
            finding commands, choosing the scene to run, and selecting where the
            project should launch.
        }

        @Steps {
            
            @Step {
                Open **Xogot > Settings** when you need to adjust the editor experience or
                install components.

                Settings include appearance, code editor behavior, 3D navigation,
                debugger, environment, running, locations, components, and AI options.
                Use the Appearance pane to choose light mode, dark mode, or the system
                appearance.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-settings.png)
            }
            
            @Step {
                Press **Command-P** to open Xogot's Command Palette.

                Use the Command Palette to quickly search files and documentation, jump
                to specific line numbers, and run commands. For a full reference, see
                <doc:Command-Palette>.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-command-palette.png)
            }
            
            @Step {
                Use the Scene Selector to choose which scene Xogot launches the next
                time you click the run button. This is similar to Godot's **Run
                Specific Scene** command.

                @Image(source: mac-tour-scene-selector.png)
            }
            @Step {
                The Target Selector is located next to the Scene Selector.

                You can run in the **Local Editor**, choose a connected device, or run
                on **Vanilla Mac** to quickly launch your project without signing or
                deploying. You can also choose other device and simulator targets, or
                choose **This Mac** to build and run a fully signed Mac app.
                
                @Image(source: mac-tour-deployment-targets.png)
            }
        }
    }
}
